'''Rhosgoch''' ({{pronunciation|Rhosgoch.ogg|Welsh pronunciation|help=no}}; meaning: 'red moor') is a small village in the north of the island of [[Anglesey]], Wales, about {{convert|3.5|mi|km}} to the south-west of [[Amlwch]].<ref name=":OS:">{{cite map |title =Anglesey|map =114|year = 2016|scale =1:50,000 |series = Landranger|publisher =Ordnance Survey |isbn = 9780319262122 }}</ref> It is in the community of [[Rhosybol]].

A short distance to the west of the village is the small lake Llyn Hafodol and a mile to the south is Anglesey's largest body of water the [[reservoir]] [[Llyn Alaw]] (''Water Lily Lake'').<ref name=":OS:" />

The village once had a station on the [[Anglesey Central Railway]].<ref>{{cite book|last1=Bridge|first1=Mike|title=Railway track diagrams; Book 4 - Midlands & North West|date=2013|publisher=Trackmaps|location=Bradford on Avon|isbn=978-0-9549866-7-4|page=37A|edition=3}}</ref> Although the tracks still exist, no train has run on them since 1993.<ref>{{cite magazine|last1=Scotchman|first1=Ian|title=Rail Route Changes|magazine=The Railway Magazine|date=June 1994|volume=140|issue=1118|page=20|publisher=IPC Media|location=London|issn=0033-8923}}</ref> Also connected to the railway, was a short south-west facing spur that led to an oil terminal. This was linked to a floating dock in the sea off of Amlwch, where super-tankers could dock in all tides and feed oil via Rhosgoch and a pipeline to [[Stanlow Oil Refinery|Stanlow oil refinery]]. This operation lasted for 16 years between 1974 and 1990.<ref>{{cite web|title=Anglesey Marine Terminal|url=http://www.amlwchhistory.co.uk/shell.html|website=www.amlwchhistory.co.uk|access-date=29 July 2017}}</ref>

The first tornado of the record-breaking [[1981 United Kingdom tornado outbreak]], an F1/T2 tornado, touched down close to Rhosgoch at around 10:19 local time on 23 November 1981.{{Citation needed|reason=Be cool if it true|date=July 2018}}

